---

Devices that hold the same swarm key form a swarm: they connect to each other,
list each other's shares and sync the [swarm folder]($link.page('docs/swarm-folder')).
Connecting a new device means giving it the swarm key and an address to dial,
safely. One-time invites do both.

# [Invite a device]($heading.id('invite'))

On a device already in the swarm:

- **App:** **Connect devices → Create invite**. The invite shows as a code and
  a QR code, with a countdown and its status; it can be copied or cancelled.
- **Command line:** `nomadfs --invite` prints the QR code in the terminal and
  the code, then waits for the other device. `--address <ip>` sets the address
  put in the code when the detected LAN address is not the right one.

An invite looks like this:

```
nomadfs://invite?addr=192.168.1.20:9001&token=<32 hex characters>
```

It is good for **one** join and expires after **10 minutes**.

# [Join with an invite]($heading.id('join'))

On the new device, open the app and go to **Connect devices**:

- paste the code under **Join with an invite** and press **Join**, or
- tap **Scan QR code**: a full-screen viewfinder opens on the rear camera and
  joins as soon as it reads a NomadFS invite.

The scanner uses the web view's `BarcodeDetector`, which Android's web view
has and WebKitGTK does not, so the button appears on phones and not on the
Linux desktop: desktops show invites, phones scan them. The app asks for camera
permission the first time.

When the join completes, each device has the other as a trusted device, the
new device has the swarm key, and it has saved the inviter's address, so either
side can reconnect after a restart.

# [What happens on the wire]($heading.id('wire'))

The inviting device opens a short-lived listener on its node port + 1. The
token in the code is 128 random bits; both sides derive a pre-shared key from
it and run a Noise `XXpsk3` handshake, so only a holder of the token gets
through. A 128-bit token resists offline guessing, and a code read off the
screen after it has been used opens nothing.

Inside that encrypted channel the devices swap signed pairing tickets (each
device's identity, transport key and addresses), and the inviter sends the
swarm key and its node port. The swarm key is never part of the code and never
appears on screen.

# [Device identities and trust]($heading.id('trust'))

Each device has an Ed25519 identity (`device.key`), separate from its transport
key. In every connection both sides prove their identity with a signature bound
to that Noise session, so a captured handshake cannot be replayed. Paired
devices are kept in an owner-only, signed trust store (`trust.store`) that pins
each device's transport key and checks it again on every handshake.

By default a device with the swarm key can connect even if it is not in the
trust store; set `require_trusted_devices = true` to accept only trusted
devices. The app's **Node & trust** section lists trusted devices (marked
online when reachable) and, below them, devices that are connected but not
paired.

From the command line:

```sh
nomadfs --devices             # list trusted devices
nomadfs --revoke <id>         # remove one, by device ID or fingerprint
nomadfs --pair                # print this device's pairing ticket and QR code
nomadfs --trust 'nomadfs://pair?…'   # trust another device's ticket
```

A revocation is a signed tombstone that is sent on to the other devices the
revoking device knows, so a removed device is dropped across the swarm, not
only on one machine. Pairing tickets from `--pair` are valid for 24 hours.

# [Without invites]($heading.id('manual'))

Devices can also share a swarm by configuration: the same `swarm_key` in each
`nomadfs.conf`, and each other's address in `bootstrap_peers`
(`"tcp://192.168.1.20:9000"`). This is the way to connect two machines that
only run the command line, since joining with an invite is done from the app.

# [Devices on different networks]($heading.id('relay'))

Each device tells the others every address it has: the home network,
Tailscale or another VPN. Devices remember them, so a phone that met the
desktop at home reaches it over Tailscale when it's away.

When none of a device's addresses answers (a home server that is only on the
home network, seen from a phone on mobile data), a device both can reach
carries the connection, for example the desktop that is on both. Nothing needs
setting up: the phone asks the devices it is connected to, and the first that
can reach the server pipes the connection through. It stays encrypted end to
end between the phone and the server; the device in the middle only passes
bytes along. All the traffic then goes through that device, so a direct path
(both on Tailscale) is still faster.

Every device involved needs NomadFS 0.3.4 or later.
